Abstract

This document defines the Cognitive Capability Model of the Memory Operating System (MOS).

A Cognitive Capability represents a fundamental semantic ability through which a cognitive system interacts with, interprets, transforms, and improves the Cognitive State Space.

Capabilities define what cognition can do.

Capabilities do not define:

transport mechanisms
software architecture
implementation language
infrastructure
model providers

All capability execution SHALL preserve MOS guarantees:

provenance
explainability
versioning
policy enforcement
transactional integrity
1. Purpose

The previous RFCs define:

RFC-0001:

The principles governing MOS.

RFC-0002:

The dynamics and evolution of cognition.

RFC-0003:

The structure and identity of Cognitive Elements.

RFC-0004:

The organization of cognition through Domains and Meta-Cognitive Elements.

This RFC defines the semantic abilities that operate upon the Cognitive State Space.

2. Capability Axiom

MOS defines:

Every cognitive action SHALL be represented through one or more Cognitive Capabilities.

No external actor SHALL directly modify canonical cognitive state.

All cognitive changes SHALL pass through the capability lifecycle.

3. Capability Architecture

The complete execution model:

                Cognitive Intent
                       │
                       ▼
              Cognitive Capability
                       │
                       ▼
            Cognitive Orchestrator
                       │
                       ▼
              Cognitive Strategy
                       │
                       ▼
              Cognitive Operation
                       │
                       ▼
            Cognitive Transaction
                       │
                       ▼
          Cognitive State Evolution

Each layer has a distinct responsibility.

4. Semantic Responsibility Model
Intent

Defines:

Why cognition should occur.

Examples:

answer a question
solve a problem
improve memory
execute a task

(Intent is formally defined in RFC-0006.)

Capability

Defines:

What cognitive ability is required.

Examples:

Recall
Learn
Plan
Reflect
Orchestrator

Defines:

Which cognitive strategies should be used.

The Orchestrator selects, combines, and supervises strategies.

Strategy

Defines:

How a capability is semantically achieved.

Examples:

Recall strategies:

Graph retrieval
Vector retrieval
Episodic reconstruction
Hybrid retrieval
Operation

Defines:

Atomic semantic transformations.

Operations are defined in RFC-0002.

Transaction

Defines:

Safe and atomic cognitive state changes.

Transactions are defined in RFC-0003.

5. Cognitive Capability Definition

A Cognitive Capability SHALL be a first-class semantic object.

A capability SHALL contain:

Identity

A globally unique identifier.

Purpose

A description of the cognitive ability provided.

Contract

A semantic contract defining:

inputs
outputs
preconditions
postconditions
constraints
policies
supported domains
explainability requirements
failure behavior
Provenance

The origin and history of the capability.

Version

All capability changes SHALL be versioned.

6. Capability Contract Model

Every capability SHALL define:

Capability

Identity

Purpose

Input Semantics

Output Semantics

Preconditions

Postconditions

Supported Domains

Required Policies

Available Strategies

Explainability Requirements

Failure Semantics
7. Capability Lifecycle

Capabilities follow:

Created

↓

Validated

↓

Published

↓

Available

↓

Executed

↓

Evaluated

↓

Improved

↓

Versioned

↓

Deprecated

Historical capability versions SHALL remain explainable.

8. Core Cognitive Capabilities

RFC-0005 defines the mandatory MOS capability set.

8.1 Observe Capability
Purpose

Acquire information.

Sources may include:

humans
agents
sensors
documents
external systems

Creates:

observations
events
source references

Observe SHALL NOT silently modify existing knowledge.

8.2 Remember Capability
Purpose

Transform observations into persistent cognition.

Lifecycle:

Observation

↓

Interpretation

↓

Entity Resolution

↓

Fact Creation

↓

Relationship Creation

↓

Provenance Assignment

↓

Memory Update
8.3 Recall Capability
Purpose

Retrieve relevant cognition from the Cognitive State Space.

Recall considers:

relevance
confidence
freshness
activation
domain membership
policies

Recall SHALL provide explanation of retrieved cognition.

8.4 Search Capability
Purpose

Locate candidate Cognitive Elements.

Supported approaches may include:

symbolic search
semantic search
graph traversal
temporal search
hybrid retrieval

Search provides candidates.

Recall determines cognitive relevance.

8.5 Explain Capability
Purpose

Provide understanding of cognitive origin and reasoning.

Explain SHALL reconstruct:

provenance
relationships
policies
transactions
versions
decisions
8.6 Reason Capability
Purpose

Generate new cognitive understanding.

May produce:

conclusions
hypotheses
relationships
interpretations

Reason SHALL preserve uncertainty.

8.7 Plan Capability
Purpose

Create future-oriented cognitive structures.

Produces:

goals
tasks
dependencies
milestones
8.8 Execute Capability
Purpose

Interact with external systems.

Execution SHALL produce:

action records
outcomes
failures
observations

External side effects SHALL be recorded.

8.9 Reflect Capability
Purpose

Improve cognitive organization.

Reflection may perform:

consolidation
contradiction detection
summarization
confidence adjustment
memory optimization
8.10 Learn Capability
Purpose

Improve future cognitive behavior.

Learning may update:

strategies
policies
models
preferences

Learning SHALL preserve previous versions.

8.11 Predict Capability
Purpose

Generate possible future states.

Predictions SHALL contain:

assumptions
confidence
provenance

Predictions SHALL NOT be represented as observations.

8.12 Govern Capability
Purpose

Apply Meta-Cognitive control.

Govern manages:

policies
permissions
trust
constraints
compliance
8.13 Monitor Capability
Purpose

Observe cognitive system health.

Measures:

quality
latency
confidence
failures
resource usage
9. Cognitive Orchestrators

A Cognitive Orchestrator is a Meta-Cognitive Element responsible for coordinating capability execution.

An Orchestrator SHALL NOT directly modify cognition.

Responsibilities include:

selecting strategies
composing strategies
scheduling execution
applying optimization policies
managing fallback strategies
evaluating execution quality

Example:

Recall Capability

↓

Recall Orchestrator

↓

Graph Strategy

+

Vector Strategy

+

Ranking Strategy

↓

Operations

↓

Transaction
10. Cognitive Strategies

Strategies are Meta-Cognitive Elements describing possible realizations of capabilities.

A Strategy SHALL contain:

identity
purpose
applicable capabilities
applicable domains
required policies
expected outcomes
limitations
performance characteristics

Example:

Hybrid Recall Strategy

Capability:

Recall


Methods:

Graph traversal

+

Vector similarity

+

Temporal filtering


Expected:

High relevance

Higher latency
11. Strategy Selection

Strategy selection SHALL consider:

intent
policies
domain context
confidence requirements
resource availability
optimization objectives

Selection SHALL be explainable.

12. Capability Composition

Capabilities MAY compose.

Example:

User Question

↓

Recall

↓

Explain

↓

Reason

↓

Answer

All composed capabilities SHALL preserve provenance.

13. Capability Explainability

Every capability execution SHALL generate an execution trace.

The trace SHALL contain:

Intent

↓

Capability

↓

Orchestrator

↓

Strategy

↓

Operations

↓

Transactions

↓

State Changes
14. Capability Security

Capabilities SHALL respect:

Governance Policies
Domain Policies
Access Controls
Trust Models

No capability bypasses governance.

15. Capability Discovery

MOS implementations SHOULD support capability discovery.

Discovery allows systems to determine:

available capabilities
versions
contracts
requirements
supported domains
strategies

This enables future agent interoperability.

16. Extension Capabilities

Implementations MAY define additional capabilities.

Examples:

Translate
Simulate
Negotiate
Diagnose
Debate
Optimize
Coordinate

Extensions SHALL follow RFC-0005 semantics.

17. Compliance

A MOS implementation complies with RFC-0005 if:

capabilities are semantic objects
capability execution follows the defined lifecycle
strategies are separated from capabilities
orchestration is explicit
all state changes occur through transactions
execution remains explainable
